89,"grapes":[["Pinot Noir",13093,"R","Pinot Noir"],["Chardonnay",10676,"W","Chardonnay"],["Meunier",10265,"R","Pinot Meunier"],["Pinot Blanc",119,"W","Pinot Blanc"],["Petit Meslier",17,"W","Petit Meslier"],["Pinot Gris",11,"G","Pinot Gris"],["Arbane",8,"W","Arbane"]],"whites_older":true,"any_name":true,"legal":["main","main","main","main","main","main","main"]},"area_km2":3589,"rocks":{"families":[["limestone",0.48],["chalk",0.28],["clay",0.11],["sand",0.11]],"points":15,"scale":"medium","names":{"limestone":"Limestone","chalk":"Chalk","clay":"Clay","sand":"Sand"}},"large":false,"reading":{"climate":[["Grown here","Pinot Noir and Chardonnay sit in their sweet spot, so the wines can show the style they are known for."]]},"winkler":["Ia",""],"terms":[["AOC / AOP","France's protected place names. A wine sold under one must follow that appellation's rules. The EU's term for the same tier is PDO, or AOP in French."],["Non-vintage, Vintage","Most Champagne is non-vintage, a blend of several years for a steady house style. In great years a house may declare a vintage and bottle wine from that harvest alone."],["Blanc de Blancs","Made only from white grapes. In Champagne that usually means 100% Chardonnay."],["Blanc de Noirs","White wine made only from black grapes. In Champagne, Pinot Noir, Meunier or both."],["Extra Brut, Brut, Demi-sec","How sweet a sparkling wine is after dosage: Extra Brut under 6 g of sugar a liter, Brut under 12, Demi-sec 32 to 50. Brut is the most common."]],"keyfacts":["The appellation requires a minimum of fifteen months before release.","After aging on the lees, riddling moves the lees into the bottle neck, disgorging removes them, and a liqueur d'expédition is added.","Vintage champagne may use no more than 80% of the wine a producer makes or buys in that year, keeping the rest for blends and reserve wines."],"style":{"notes":["Pinot Noir gives Champagne body and structure, Chardonnay brings freshness, and Meunier adds roundness.","A dosage of sugar and wine sets the sweetness, and today it is used mostly to fine-tune the sense of acidity."]},"blurb":{"text":"An appellation in northeast France for sparkling wine, made by secondary fermentation in the bottle. Pinot Noir, Meunier and Chardonnay make almost all of it, with small amounts of Pinot Blanc, Pinot Gris, Arbane and Petit Meslier. It became linked with royalty and luxury."},"study":true,"levels":[2,3],"summary":{"places":66,"kinds":[["cru village",59],["region",5],["PDO",2]],"grapes":[["Pinot Noir","red",2]],"with_rules":2,"gst":[13.5,14.7],"best":["france/cote-des-blancs","france/rose-des-riceys","france/vallee-de-la-marne","france/montagne-de-reims"]},"sources":[{"name":"Wikidata sitelinks and Wikimedia page views","url":"https://www.wikidata.org"},{"name":"Candiago et al. 2022, A geospatial inventory of regulatory information for wine protected designations of origin in Europe, Scientific Data (product specifications as of 2021)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01513-0"},{"name":"INAO, the appellation's cahier des charges, chapter V (grapes and their shares), from the Bulletin officiel du ministère de l'Agriculture (Licence Ouverte)","url":"https://www.inao.gouv.fr/rechercher-un-produit"},{"name":"Fick and Hijmans 2017, WorldClim 2.1 monthly averages for 1970 to 2000, 2.5 minute grid (about 4.5 km)","url":"https://www.worldclim.org/data/worldclim21.html"},{"name":"Beck et al. 2023, High-resolution (1 km) Koppen-Geiger maps, Scientific Data (1991 to 2020)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02549-6"},{"name":"Winkler, Cook, Kliewer and Lider 1974, General Viticulture, University of California Press (heat summation regions)","url":null},{"name":"Jones 2010, Climate, grapes, and wine: structure and suitability in a variable and changing climate, VIII International Terroir Congress (which grapes suit which growing season temperatures)","url":"https://ives-openscience.eu/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Climate_Grape_Wine_Jones.pdf"},{"name"